&lt;p&gt;↓&lt;br&gt;
npm i react-router-dom&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
BrowserRouter&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Routes&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Route&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Different React Pages&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My portfolio repository, &lt;a href="https://github.com/Sumit884-byte/rsume-site" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;rsume-site&lt;/a&gt;, is a React application built around a glassmorphic bento grid. The interesting part is not just the translucent cards. It is how the page separates its presentation from a generated content file, while keeping most of the interface in a straightforward component tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A small frontend stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The package configuration uses React 19, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS 4, Framer Motion, and Lucide icons. There is no application server in the implementation described here. The page imports its content from &lt;code&gt;src/data/auto-content.json&lt;/code&gt;, rather than fetching the project list during rendering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;src/App.tsx&lt;/code&gt;, the root component composes a background, floating decorations, a glass shell, and individual cards. A reusable &lt;code&gt;GlassCard&lt;/code&gt; wraps its children in a motion element with an opacity and vertical entrance animation. Each card supplies its own delay, so the layout can appear in stages without repeating the animation configuration everywhere.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cards cover a hero, three featured skill categories, projects, an Arka link, and contact information. The hero delegates its title and floating code brackets to separate components. That keeps those visual effects out of the main layout code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Project data is separate from project presentation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I find the project selection logic worth making explicit. The application copies and sorts the project array, placing the configured Arka repository first and ordering the remaining entries by update time. The phone-shaped project previews then exclude Arka and take only two projects. A separate “View all” link points to the GitHub profile.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those phone previews are illustrative UI compositions, not screenshots of the linked applications. They use gradients, translucent blocks, a project title, and a hover animation. This distinction matters when describing what a visitor is actually seeing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The README documents a refresh command that updates the generated JSON:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm run sync
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It also says GitHub Actions refreshes projects daily and on main-branch pushes that touch the sync script. The page therefore consumes a saved snapshot of project metadata, not a live feed on every visit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Running the frontend
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The README lists these development commands:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm run dev
npm run build
npm run lint
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The package file confirms that the production build runs &lt;code&gt;tsc -b &amp;amp;&amp;amp; vite build&lt;/code&gt;. It also provides &lt;code&gt;npm run preview&lt;/code&gt;. The README does not include a complete dependency-installation walkthrough, so those commands should not be mistaken for a fully documented setup sequence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The limits of this version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some content is intentionally fixed in code, including the featured skill categories and email contact. The LinkedIn QR image comes from an external QR-generation service. That introduces a network dependency even though the project data itself is bundled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would describe this version as a focused portfolio frontend, not a CMS or a real-time GitHub dashboard. This walkthrough is based on source inspection, not a fresh build or performance benchmark. The concrete takeaway is architectural: a generated data file can sit behind an expressive React layout without making the rendering layer responsible for synchronization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What carries over
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Angular concept&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;React equivalent / relevance&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Component‑based architecture&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Both frameworks revolve around reusable components. In Angular you declare a component with &lt;code&gt;@Component&lt;/code&gt;, in React you write a function or class that returns JSX. The mental model of “a UI piece with its own template and logic” stays the same.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dependency injection (DI) basics&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular’s built‑in DI container is more opinionated, but the idea of providing a service instance to a component persists. In React you achieve the same with the Context API or custom hooks (&lt;code&gt;useContext&lt;/code&gt;). The pattern of “pulling a dependency from a shared source instead of &lt;code&gt;new&lt;/code&gt;‑ing it” is unchanged.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Unidirectional data flow&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular’s &lt;code&gt;@Input&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;@Output&lt;/code&gt; pattern maps to React’s props‑down, events‑up model. You still pass data from parent to child via props and notify the parent via callbacks (&lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt;, custom handlers).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lifecycle awareness&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular’s &lt;code&gt;ngOnInit&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ngOnChanges&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ngOnDestroy&lt;/code&gt; have React counterparts in &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; (with appropriate dependency arrays) and cleanup functions. The need to run side‑effects at the right moment is identical.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Template syntax for binding&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular’s interpolation &lt;code&gt;{{value}}&lt;/code&gt; and property binding &lt;code&gt;[src]="url"&lt;/code&gt; become JSX expressions &lt;code&gt;{value}&lt;/code&gt; and attribute values &lt;code&gt;{url}&lt;/code&gt;. The underlying principle—embedding JavaScript expressions in markup—remains.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Routing concepts&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular Router’s route definitions (&lt;code&gt;path&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;component&lt;/code&gt;) map to React Router’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Route path="" element={...} /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. Guarding routes, lazy loading modules, and param extraction are all present, just with different APIs.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Testing mindset&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unit testing components with Jasmine/Karma translates to testing React components with Jest and React Testing Library. The focus on isolated rendering, mocking services, and asserting DOM output does not change.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;State management patterns&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NgRx’s store, actions, reducers, selectors are conceptually identical to Redux (or Redux Toolkit) in React. If you already use NgRx, the mental model of a single source of truth and immutable updates will feel familiar.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's new
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is a tiny “click counter” implemented in both frameworks. The Angular version uses a component class, template, and two‑way binding. The React version uses a functional component with hooks.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Angular (TypeScript)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Component&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@angular/core'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@Component&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;selector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'app-counter'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;template&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`
&amp;lt;button (click)="increment()"&amp;gt;Clicked {{ count }} times&amp;lt;/button&amp;gt;
`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CounterComponent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// React (JavaScript)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Clicked&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;times&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


What changed and why
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;File structure&lt;/strong&gt; – Angular encourages a “feature folder” with separate &lt;code&gt;.ts&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.html&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;.scss&lt;/code&gt; files. React co‑locates JSX, logic, and styling (CSS Modules, styled‑components) in a single file or adjacent files, reducing the number of moving parts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Templates vs JSX&lt;/strong&gt; – Angular’s HTML template is parsed by the Angular compiler; you write plain HTML with Angular directives. React’s JSX is JavaScript syntax sugar; every expression is compiled by Babel/TSX, giving you full JavaScript power inside markup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State handling&lt;/strong&gt; – Angular’s component class fields (&lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt;) are mutable; change detection runs automatically. In React you must treat state as immutable and call the setter (&lt;code&gt;setCount&lt;/code&gt;) to trigger a re‑render. This explicitness avoids hidden side‑effects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Event binding&lt;/strong&gt; – Angular’s &lt;code&gt;(click)&lt;/code&gt; syntax is declarative and automatically binds &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;. React’s &lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt; is a prop that receives a function reference; you manage the lexical scope yourself (arrow functions or &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Change detection&lt;/strong&gt; – Angular runs a zone‑based change detection cycle after every async event. React uses a virtual DOM diff on each state update, which can be more predictable but requires you to batch updates when needed (e.g., &lt;code&gt;unstable_batchedUpdates&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;startTransition&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Common pitfalls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pitfall&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it happens for Angular vets&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How to avoid it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutating state directly&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In Angular you can assign &lt;code&gt;this.someObj.prop = 5&lt;/code&gt; and the framework picks it up. In React you must create a new object (&lt;code&gt;setObj(prev =&amp;gt; ({...prev, prop: 5}))&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Always treat props and state as immutable. Use spread syntax or utility libraries (&lt;code&gt;immer&lt;/code&gt;) to produce new objects.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Forgetting the dependency array in &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular’s &lt;code&gt;ngOnInit&lt;/code&gt; runs once, and &lt;code&gt;ngOnChanges&lt;/code&gt; runs on input changes automatically. In React, &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs after every render unless you pass a dependency array.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Add an empty array &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; for “run once” effects, or list specific variables to re‑run on change.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Using &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt; in functional components&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular classes rely on &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt; for services and properties. React functional components have no &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Switch to hooks (&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useContext&lt;/code&gt;). If you need a class component, remember that &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt; refers to the component instance, not a DI container.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assuming Angular’s DI will work in React&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular’s &lt;code&gt;@Injectable&lt;/code&gt; services are provided by the injector tree. React has no built‑in injector.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use Context to provide global services, or keep a separate service layer (plain classes) and import them where needed.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Overusing JSX for complex logic&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular templates discourage heavy logic; you move it to the component class. JSX can embed any JS, tempting you to write loops or conditionals directly in markup.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keep JSX declarative; extract complex calculations into helper functions or custom hooks.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Neglecting key props in lists&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular’s &lt;code&gt;*ngFor&lt;/code&gt; tracks by identity automatically. React requires a &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; prop for each list item to preserve identity.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Always provide a stable &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; (id or index if truly static).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Your 30‑day plan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 1 – Foundations
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Set up the toolchain&lt;/strong&gt; – Install Node ≥ 18, run &lt;code&gt;npx create-react-app my‑app --template typescript&lt;/code&gt;, explore &lt;code&gt;src/index.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;App.tsx&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Learn JSX basics&lt;/strong&gt; – Convert a simple Angular template (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;{{title}}&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;) into JSX (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;{title}&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;). Practice embedding expressions, ternaries, and array maps.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State with hooks&lt;/strong&gt; – Implement a counter, a toggle, and a form using &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;. Compare with Angular’s &lt;code&gt;[(ngModel)]&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project&lt;/strong&gt; – Build a “Todo list” component that adds, toggles, and removes items. Use local component state only.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 2 – Component architecture &amp;amp; routing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Component composition&lt;/strong&gt; – Create reusable UI primitives (Button, Card) as separate files. Use props for configuration, mirroring Angular Input bindings.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Context API&lt;/strong&gt; – Build a simple theme provider (&lt;code&gt;ThemeContext&lt;/code&gt;) and consume it in a nested component. Relate this to Angular’s &lt;code&gt;providedIn: 'root'&lt;/code&gt; services.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React Router v6&lt;/strong&gt; – Install &lt;code&gt;react-router-dom&lt;/code&gt;, define routes (&lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/about&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/todos/:id&lt;/code&gt;). Implement navigation with &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Link&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and route params with &lt;code&gt;useParams&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project&lt;/strong&gt; – Extend the Todo app into a multi‑page app: a list view (&lt;code&gt;/todos&lt;/code&gt;) and a detail view (&lt;code&gt;/todos/:id&lt;/code&gt;). Add a navigation bar.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 3 – Data fetching &amp;amp; state management
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fetching with &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; – Pull a public JSON placeholder list, handle loading/error states. Compare to Angular’s &lt;code&gt;HttpClient&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Introduce Redux Toolkit&lt;/strong&gt; – Install &lt;code&gt;@reduxjs/toolkit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;react-redux&lt;/code&gt;. Create a slice for todos, dispatch actions from components, and select state with &lt;code&gt;useSelector&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Async thunks&lt;/strong&gt; – Write an async thunk to load todos from the API, handling pending/fulfilled/rejected cases.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project&lt;/strong&gt; – Refactor the Todo app to store its data in Redux, persisting to &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt; via a middleware. Add a “Refresh from server” button.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 4 – Advanced patterns &amp;amp; testing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Custom hooks&lt;/strong&gt; – Extract the data‑fetching logic into a &lt;code&gt;useTodos&lt;/code&gt; hook. Compare to Angular services.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Performance tricks&lt;/strong&gt; – Use &lt;code&gt;React.memo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; to avoid unnecessary renders. Identify a component that re‑renders too often with the React DevTools Profiler.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Testing with Jest &amp;amp; React Testing Library&lt;/strong&gt; – Write unit tests for a component (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;TodoItem&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;), mocking a context provider and asserting DOM output.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project&lt;/strong&gt; – Add a “search” feature to the Todo list using a debounced input (&lt;code&gt;useDebounce&lt;/code&gt; hook). Write a test that verifies the filtered results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the end of the month you should have a small but complete React codebase that mirrors the structure of a typical Angular app, but uses idiomatic React patterns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I replace Angular services in React?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the Context API to provide a singleton-like object, or simply export a class instance and import it where needed. For reactive streams, consider RxJS directly or &lt;code&gt;useObservable&lt;/code&gt; hooks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Do I still need TypeScript?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Create‑React‑App’s TypeScript template gives you the same static typing benefits you enjoy in Angular. All component props, state, and context values should be typed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Should I learn Redux before React Router?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Both are independent libraries. Start with routing to get comfortable navigating between pages, then add Redux (or Redux Toolkit) when you need shared state beyond simple prop drilling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is there an equivalent to Angular’s &lt;code&gt;ngIf&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;ngFor&lt;/code&gt;?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. In JSX you use JavaScript expressions: &lt;code&gt;{condition &amp;amp;&amp;amp; &amp;lt;Component/&amp;gt;}&lt;/code&gt; for &lt;code&gt;ngIf&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;{items.map(item =&amp;gt; &amp;lt;Component key={item.id} {...item} /&amp;gt;)}&lt;/code&gt; for &lt;code&gt;ngFor&lt;/code&gt;. The logic lives in JavaScript rather than a template syntax.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keep going
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;See what already transfers:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/switch-check?pair=angular-to-react&amp;amp;utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=community&amp;amp;utm_campaign=blog-30-day-plan&amp;amp;utm_content=angular-developer-learning-react-what-carries-over-what-s-new-30" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;take the free Angular → React readiness check&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/switch/angular-to-react" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The full Angular → React roadmap, topic by topic&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Related: &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/blog/react-developer-learning-next-js-what-carries-over-what-s-new-30-day-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React developer learning Next.js: what carries over, what's new, 30-day plan&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Related: &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/blog/migrating-secrets-management-aws-parameter-store-vs-azure-key-vault" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Migrating Secrets Management: AWS Parameter Store vs Azure Key Vault&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/blog/angular-developer-learning-react-what-carries-over-what-s-new-30-day-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switchbyskill.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have gone back to an app that I built using Spotify API because they have messed around with all sorts of things a basic app relies on, but that's another article!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The infinite loading hook
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Something I was pleased with when returning though was how I had implemented infinite scrolling for the playlists and playlist tracks. Essentially I have used TanStack-Query's &lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/query/v3/docs/framework/react/reference/useInfiniteQuery" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;useInfiniteQuery()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; to collect 'pages' of data. A page can essentially be thought of as an object that has various properties including &lt;code&gt;offset&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;limit&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;next&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;previous&lt;/code&gt;, and of course the &lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt; themselves. It tracks where abouts you are within the data collection. The fetching is quite confusing when you read it without checking out the documentation linked above but essentially the functions that you define for getNextPageParam etc are used internally by the TanStack query function and the merged function just makes sure your items are all in one array (&lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt;). I added some JSDocs to remind me of what is going on so I'll just share the whole hook and then show how I've used it in my project (isLoggedIn() is just a simple check but stops query functions from trying to execute if it's definitely going to fail and it's called use*&lt;em&gt;Paginated&lt;/em&gt;*Fetch because it started life being used with next/prev buttons btw) -&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//I've added the rest operator so that we can pass in extra arguments if needed, such as an id for using it with the playlist tracks functionality&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useInfiniteQuery&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@tanstack/react-query'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isLoggedIn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'../services/apiSpotify'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// fetchFunction(pageOffset, limit, ...args) =&amp;gt; returns a Spotify page object { items, next, previous, limit, offset, ... }&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
* @template T
* @typedef {Object} SpotifyPage
* @property {string} href - A link to the Web API endpoint returning the full result of the request.
* @property {T[]} items - The requested data items.
* @property {number} limit - The maximum number of items in the response (as set in the query or by default).
* @property {string | null} next - URL to the next page of items, or null if none.
* @property {number} offset - The offset of the items returned (as set in the query or by default).
* @property {string | null} previous - URL to the previous page of items, or null if none.
* @property {number} total - The total number of items available to return.
* /
/**
* @typedef {Object} RawPageObject
* @property {number} offset - The index of the first item in the Page object relative to the entire collection of items
* @property {number} limit - The maximum number of items in the Page object ( namely items from offset to offset + limit) 
*/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
* A function that fetches a page of data from the Spotify API, which can optionally take additional arguments as an array
*
* @template T
* @template {any[]} [TArgs=any[]]
* @callback FetchingFunction
* @param {number} offset - The offset for pagination.
* @param {number} limit - The number of items to fetch.
* @param {TArgs} [args] - Array of additional arguments passed from usePaginatedFetch.
* @returns {Promise&amp;lt;SpotifyPage&amp;lt;T&amp;gt;&amp;gt;} - A promise that resolves to a Spotify page object.
*/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
* @template T
* @typedef {Object} UsePaginatedFetchResult
* @property {SpotifyPage&amp;lt;T&amp;gt; | undefined} data - Merged page object containing flattened items across all loaded pages.
* @property {(direction: number) =&amp;gt; Promise&amp;lt;void&amp;gt;} getNextPrev - Fetches the next page when direction &amp;gt; 0, or the previous page when direction &amp;lt; 0.
* @property {boolean} hasNext - Whether a next page is available to fetch.
* @property {boolean} hasPrevious - Whether a previous page is available to fetch.
* @property {boolean} isLoading - True during the initial load or any subsequent page fetch.
* @property {import('@tanstack/react-query').UseInfiniteQueryResult['refetch']} refetch - Function to manually refetch all loaded pages.
*/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
* Custom hook that implements paginated fetching using tanstack-query's useInfiniteQuery. It works alongside the useIntersection custom hook to enable infinite scrolling functionality. This is not for use with cursor pagination!!
*
* @template T
* @template {any[]} [TArgs=any[]]
* @param {FetchingFunction} fetchFunction - The api calling function, for example getUserPlaylists or getPlaylistTracks
* @param {SpotifyPage | RawPageObject | null} initialState - offset defaults to 0, limit defaults to 20
* @param {...TArgs} args - Additional arguments passed through to the fetchFunction, for example for Playlist tracks we pass in the playlistId
* @returns {UsePaginatedFetchResult&amp;lt;T&amp;gt;} - An object containing the merged data (which has an items property that contains all of the pages items for example all the track objects in a playlist), pagination functions, and loading state.
*/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;usePaginatedFetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fetchFunction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;initialOffset&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;offset&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
